     "result_text_tail": "er premium.\n- **The deposit is now shown as insurance.** It is lost in three of the four order scenarios, and it's worth paying only if the IC sees at least a 14% chance that it prevents losing a customer's allocation for a year.\n\n\"Buy overflow only, with no deposit\" is shown as a close second. It would be the better choice if a later installation slot turns out to be easy to get, which is why checking that with the supplier is the first next step.\n\n**Choices I made for you:**\n- Navy theme (the default).\n- I used the 2029 signed minimums, not the forecast, to size the contribution at stake ($4.2M a year).\n\n**Data:** all 68 numbers come from the briefing pack (29) or are calculated from it (39). Seven of the calculated ones are estimates, and each shows its formula on its slide. No placeholders are left. There were no web sources, so the step that re-opens sources to verify them was skipped.\n\n**Two things the IC should know:**\n- **Missing costs:** the pack has no 2027\u201328 operating cash flow, construction inflation, specialty validation cost or salvage value, so all of these are left out.\n- **Rebid risk:** the pack gives customers the right to rebid, not how likely they are to use it, so the $4.2M is an upper bound.\n\nI can switch the theme or turn the June 2027 review into a one-page decision memo.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`; the editable spec and the script that builds it are in `./work/`:\n- pelican-capacity.pptx (18 slides, 11 with content)\n- pelican-capacity-sources.xlsx"
    }
